***REMOVED*** Python Google Style Guide

Whenever I'm writing Python code, enforce the following rules based on the Google Python Style Guide:

***REMOVED******REMOVED*** ✅ Code Style & Naming
- Use snake_case for functions and variables
- Use CapWords (PascalCase) for class names
- Use ALL_CAPS for constants
- Use self._private_method() naming for internal methods

***REMOVED******REMOVED*** 📄 Docstrings
- Every public module, class, and function must have a docstring using Google-style formatting:
  - Start with a 1-line summary
  - Include Args:, Returns:, and Raises: sections as needed
  - Use triple double-quotes """

***REMOVED******REMOVED*** 🧪 Testing (TDD Support)
- If I define a new function or class, ask me whether I want to generate a test
- Prefer pytest style for tests
- Tests should live in a mirrored tests/ directory and be named test_<module>.py

***REMOVED******REMOVED*** 🧼 Code Quality
- All functions must include type hints
- Enforce max line length of 100 characters
- Favor clear, readable, and modular functions (ideally <40 lines)
- Prefer exceptions over return codes for error handling
- Suggest using logging instead of print

***REMOVED******REMOVED*** 📦 Imports & Project Structure
- Use absolute imports, not relative (e.g., from my_package.module import MyClass)
- Order imports: Standard library → Third-party → Local modules
- Organize each feature or module into its own file, named with snake_case

***REMOVED******REMOVED*** 🛠️ Tools
- Use black for formatting, ruff or pylint for linting, and mypy for type checking
- Use pyproject.toml as the central config when applicable

***REMOVED******REMOVED*** 🔍 Additional Guidelines
- Offer to automatically fix formatting or docstring style issues
- When reviewing code, proactively flag anything that breaks Google's conventions
